%%report data-quality-finding-by-severity title: Data Quality Findings by severity entity: DataQualityFinding chart: pie x: severity y: records help: A governed defect against an operational record, assigned for correction and retained for recurring data-quality dashboards. How data quality findings divide across severity, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(severity::text, '(unset)') AS severity, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_data_quality_finding WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report data-quality-finding-lifecycle title: Data Quality Finding lifecycle entity: DataQualityFinding chart: bar x: state y: records help: A governed defect against an operational record, assigned for correction and retained for recurring data-quality dashboards. Where data quality findings sit in their state machine, in the order the diagram draws it; a state nothing has reached shows as zero. sql: SELECT d.state, COALESCE(c.records, 0) AS records FROM (SELECT 'open' AS state, 0 AS position UNION ALL SELECT 'assigned', 1 UNION ALL SELECT 'remediating', 2 UNION ALL SELECT 'resolved', 3 UNION ALL SELECT 'accepted_risk', 4) d LEFT JOIN (SELECT status AS state, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_data_quality_finding WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1) c ON c.state = d.state ORDER BY d.position
%%report revenue-movement-by-movement-type title: Revenue Movements by movement type entity: RevenueMovement chart: pie x: movement_type y: records help: A dated movement in recurring revenue, recording both prior and new recurring amounts for expansion and churn analysis. How revenue movements divide across movement type, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(movement_type::text, '(unset)') AS movement_type, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_revenue_movement WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report revenue-movement-previous-mrr-by-movement-type title: Total previous mrr by movement type entity: RevenueMovement chart: bar x: movement_type y: total_previous_mrr help: A dated movement in recurring revenue, recording both prior and new recurring amounts for expansion and churn analysis. Revenue Movements added up on previous mrr, split by movement type, to show where the quantity sits. sql: SELECT COALESCE(movement_type::text, '(unset)') AS movement_type, ROUND(SUM(previous_mrr)::numeric, 2) AS total_previous_mrr FROM bus_revenue_movement WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC NULLS LAST
%%report revenue-movement-previous-mrr-per-month title: Average previous mrr per month entity: RevenueMovement chart: line x: month y: average_previous_mrr help: A dated movement in recurring revenue, recording both prior and new recurring amounts for expansion and churn analysis. The average previous mrr of revenue movements created each month, to see whether the typical record is growing or shrinking. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, ROUND(AVG(previous_mrr)::numeric, 2) AS average_previous_mrr FROM bus_revenue_movement WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
%%report revenue-movement-largest-by-previous-mrr title: The ten largest revenue movements by previous mrr entity: RevenueMovement chart: bar x: record y: previous_mrr help: A dated movement in recurring revenue, recording both prior and new recurring amounts for expansion and churn analysis. The records with the highest previous mrr; worth reading for outliers and for the few that carry most of the total. sql: SELECT movement_type::text AS record, previous_mrr FROM bus_revenue_movement WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND previous_mrr IS NOT NULL ORDER BY previous_mrr DESC LIMIT 10
%%report revenue-movement-new-mrr-by-movement-type title: Total new mrr by movement type entity: RevenueMovement chart: bar x: movement_type y: total_new_mrr help: A dated movement in recurring revenue, recording both prior and new recurring amounts for expansion and churn analysis. Revenue Movements added up on new mrr, split by movement type, to show where the quantity sits. sql: SELECT COALESCE(movement_type::text, '(unset)') AS movement_type, ROUND(SUM(new_mrr)::numeric, 2) AS total_new_mrr FROM bus_revenue_movement WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC NULLS LAST
%%report revenue-movement-new-mrr-per-month title: Average new mrr per month entity: RevenueMovement chart: line x: month y: average_new_mrr help: A dated movement in recurring revenue, recording both prior and new recurring amounts for expansion and churn analysis. The average new mrr of revenue movements created each month, to see whether the typical record is growing or shrinking. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, ROUND(AVG(new_mrr)::numeric, 2) AS average_new_mrr FROM bus_revenue_movement WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
%%report revenue-movement-top-currency title: Revenue Movements: the most common currency entity: RevenueMovement chart: bar x: currency y: records help: A dated movement in recurring revenue, recording both prior and new recurring amounts for expansion and churn analysis. The ten most frequent values of currency among revenue movements; free text that repeats is a list the model could close. sql: SELECT COALESCE(currency, '(blank)') AS currency, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_revenue_movement WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report lead-routing-event-by-routing-type title: Lead Routing Events by routing type entity: LeadRoutingEvent chart: pie x: routing_type y: records help: A history of lead assignment decisions, response speed and acceptance to support routing effectiveness and fairness audits. How lead routing events divide across routing type, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(routing_type::text, '(unset)') AS routing_type, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_lead_routing_event WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report lead-routing-event-by-outcome title: Lead Routing Events by outcome entity: LeadRoutingEvent chart: pie x: outcome y: records help: A history of lead assignment decisions, response speed and acceptance to support routing effectiveness and fairness audits. How lead routing events divide across outcome, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(outcome::text, '(unset)') AS outcome, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_lead_routing_event WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report lead-routing-event-response-minutes-by-routing-type title: Total response minutes by routing type entity: LeadRoutingEvent chart: bar x: routing_type y: total_response_minutes help: A history of lead assignment decisions, response speed and acceptance to support routing effectiveness and fairness audits. Lead Routing Events added up on response minutes, split by routing type, to show where the quantity sits. sql: SELECT COALESCE(routing_type::text, '(unset)') AS routing_type, ROUND(SUM(response_minutes)::numeric, 2) AS total_response_minutes FROM bus_lead_routing_event WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC NULLS LAST
